{"type": "FeatureCollection", "metadata": {"note": "The Burlington's Chicago-Twin Cities 'River Line' along the east bank of the Mississippi, and the route of the Twin Cities Zephyrs. Built not by the CB&Q itself but by the Chicago, Burlington and Northern Railroad, which opened in 1886 running northwest out of Oregon, Illinois to the state line and then north up the river through Savanna, East Dubuque, Prairie du Chien and La Crosse, crossing to the Minnesota bank and continuing through Winona and Red Wing into St. Paul -- about 300 scenic miles Savanna to St. Paul. The CB&Q absorbed the CB&N in 1899. From April 21 1935 this was the road of the Twin Cities Zephyrs, a pair of daily streamlined daytime trains that ran until 1971. GEOMETRY IS A REAL TRACE: a shortest-path route over OpenStreetMap ways tagged railway=rail, usage=main, with the cost of any way not operated by BNSF (the CB&Q's successor) multiplied by 12 so the router stays on the Burlington's own iron rather than the parallel ex-Milwaukee Road line, which shadows this corridor on the far bank for much of its length and which an unweighted shortest path does take. The result runs 100% on BNSF track, entirely within the BNSF Chicago, Aurora, Saint Croix and St. Paul Subdivisions -- the ex-CB&Q route. Simplified to a 30 m Douglas-Peucker tolerance. Clean of this repo's two known tracing defects: zero >120-degree reversals and zero revisit pairs. Passes within 3.4 km of Chicago, Aurora, Savanna, East Dubuque, Prairie du Chien, La Crosse, Winona, Red Wing and St. Paul.", "sources": ["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chicago,_Burlington_and_Quincy_Railroad (CB&Q system exceeded 12,000 route miles in 14 states by the 1920s; Great Northern/Northern Pacific held nearly all Burlington stock by 1901; Burlington Northern merger March 2 1970)", "https://www.encyclopediadubuque.org/index.php/CHICAGO,_BURLINGTON_AND_NORTHERN_RAILWAY_COMPANY and https://www.american-rails.com/cbq.html (Chicago, Burlington & Northern built the Mississippi River line, opened 1886, running from Oregon, Illinois northwest to the state line then north along the river to the Twin Cities -- about 300 miles Savanna to St. Paul via La Crosse and Prescott; CB&Q absorbed the CB&N in 1899)", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twin_Zephyr (Twin Cities Zephyrs introduced April 21 1935 as a pair of daily streamlined daytime trains Chicago-Twin Cities; second, larger trainsets delivered November 1936; ran until 1971)", "OpenStreetMap via Overpass (railway=rail, usage=main, bbox 41.55/-93.40 to 45.10/-87.50), retrieved 2026-08-25; base data (c) OpenStreetMap contributors, ODbL"], "not_yet_sourced": "All frequencies are estimated. The 1886 CB&N line left the Chicago-Quincy main at Oregon, Illinois; this trace instead follows the modern BNSF routing out of Chicago through Aurora, which is how the Zephyrs ran but is not the 1886 alignment for the first ~100 miles. Terminal trackage in Chicago and St. Paul is modern.", "pulled": "20260825"}, "features": [{"type": "Feature", "properties": {"company": "Chicago, Burlington and Quincy Railroad", "line": "Chicago, Burlington and Quincy: Chicago \u2013 Twin Cities", "id": "cbq-chicago-twin-cities", "color": "#8E1B3C", "mode": "intercity_rail", "avg_speed_kmh": 72, "speed_source": "estimated: fast river-grade main line, later Zephyr territory; unsourced", "segments": [{"start": 1886, "end": 1899, "headway_min": 360, "note": "Opened 1886 by the Chicago, Burlington and Northern Railroad, running up the east bank of the Mississippi from Savanna through East Dubuque, Prairie du Chien and La Crosse before crossing to the Minnesota side for Winona, Red Wing and St. Paul; frequency estimated/unsourced"}, {"start": 1899, "end": 1935, "headway_min": 240, "note": "The CB&Q absorbed the Chicago, Burlington and Northern in 1899, making the river line its own Twin Cities route and a direct competitor to the Milwaukee Road and North Western for the Chicago-St. Paul trade; frequency estimated/unsourced"}, {"start": 1935, "end": 1970, "headway_min": 180, "note": "From April 21 1935 the road of the Twin Cities Zephyrs, a pair of daily streamlined daytime trains; the original three-car sets proved too small and larger trainsets followed in November 1936; frequency estimated/unsourced"}, {"start": 1970, "end": 1971, "headway_min": 180, "note": "Merged into Burlington Northern March 2 1970; the Twin Cities Zephyr ran until Amtrak took over intercity passenger service in 1971; frequency estimated/unsourced"}], "geometry_repairs": [{"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-87.68136, 41.85892], "to": [-87.96575, 41.79566], "chord_mi": 15.0, "traced_mi": 15.3, "snap_m": [1, 1], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["BNSF Chicago Subdivision (15.3 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}], "geometry_quality": "Straight-line repair 2026-08-29: 1 ruled segment totalling 15 mi replaced with OpenStreetMap-traced rail geometry (see geometry_repairs for the OSM lines each one followed)."}, "geometry": {"type": "LineString", "coordinates": [[-87.63747, 41.86747], [-87.63712, 41.86184], [-87.63765, 41.86091], [-87.63873, 41.86042], [-87.6732, 41.85997], [-87.6813553, 41.8589168], [-87.682451, 41.8586472], [-87.6866272, 41.8573656], [-87.6879271, 41.8570411], [-87.7441, 41.8444684], [-87.7465382, 41.8440907], [-87.7543984, 41.8433294], [-87.7565187, 41.8429358], [-87.7623699, 41.8416309], [-87.7660153, 41.8407784], [-87.7684736, 41.8399738], [-87.7720242, 41.838646], [-87.7740264, 41.8379609], [-87.7804745, 41.8362768], [-87.8722238, 41.8156576], [-87.9545411, 41.7970793], [-87.958081, 41.796297], [-87.960362, 41.795905], [-87.962435, 41.795715], [-87.9657477, 41.7956558], [-88.03972, 41.7955], [-88.06434, 41.79833], [-88.07047, 41.7982], [-88.103, 41.7925], [-88.12294, 41.7826], [-88.13095, 41.78039], [-88.22496, 41.77761], [-88.29485, 41.76728], [-88.30067, 41.76599], [-88.30346, 41.76482], [-88.30541, 41.76332], [-88.30841, 41.7596], [-88.